---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-osteomalacia`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Osteomalacia',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Osteomalacia', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'M83', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Osteomalacia', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Osteomalacia: The Adult Bone-Softening Disease"
  description="Osteomalacia is rickets in the adult skeleton — soft, painful bone from severe vitamin D deficiency. How it differs from osteoporosis, the tell-tale signs, and how it is treated."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-osteomalacia", label: "Vitamin D & Osteomalacia" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Osteomalacia</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Osteomalacia is what rickets becomes when it strikes a skeleton that has already stopped
      growing. The growth plates are closed, so bones do not bow — instead, newly remodelled bone
      fails to mineralise and the skeleton becomes soft, achy, and prone to a characteristic kind of
      fracture. It is frequently missed, because its deep bone pain and muscle weakness are easy to
      write off as fibromyalgia, arthritis, or simply ageing.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Osteomalacia vs osteoporosis</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      These are constantly confused, and the distinction matters because the treatment differs.
      <strong>Osteoporosis</strong> is a loss of bone <em>quantity</em> — there is less bone, but
      what remains is normally mineralised, and it is usually painless until a fracture happens.
      <strong>Osteomalacia</strong> is a defect of bone <em>quality</em> — there is a normal amount
      of bone matrix (osteoid), but it is inadequately hardened with mineral, and it typically hurts.
      A DEXA scan can show low density in both; the biochemistry and symptoms separate them. Read our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-and-osteoporosis" class="text-amber-700 underline">osteoporosis page</a> for the contrast.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The tell-tale features</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Diffuse bone pain</strong> — often in the hips, pelvis, ribs, and lower back, worse with pressure.</li>
      <li><strong>Proximal muscle weakness</strong> — difficulty rising from a chair or climbing stairs, a waddling gait.</li>
      <li><strong>Looser zones (pseudofractures)</strong> — ribbon-like lucencies on X-ray at the femoral neck, pubic rami, and scapula, nearly pathognomonic.</li>
      <li><strong>Biochemistry</strong> — low 25(OH)D (often &lt; 10 ng/mL), low-normal calcium and phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, and elevated PTH.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who is at risk</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Osteomalacia requires <em>severe, prolonged</em> deficiency — not the mild insufficiency common
      in the general population. Risk concentrates in people with fat malabsorption
      (<a href="/vitamin-d-and-celiac-disease" class="text-amber-700 underline">coeliac disease</a>,
      Crohn's, <a href="/vitamin-d-and-bariatric-surgery" class="text-amber-700 underline">bariatric surgery</a>),
      chronic kidney or liver disease, minimal sun exposure combined with dark skin, and long-term use
      of anticonvulsants that accelerate vitamin D breakdown (see
      <a href="/vitamin-d-drug-interactions" class="text-amber-700 underline">drug interactions</a>).
      A rarer cause is tumour-induced osteomalacia, driven by phosphate wasting rather than vitamin D.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Treatment and recovery</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Nutritional osteomalacia responds dramatically to repletion. High-dose vitamin D — commonly a
      loading course followed by maintenance, with calcium — corrects the biochemistry over weeks and
      the bone pain and weakness over one to several months as the osteoid finally mineralises. Where
      malabsorption is the cause, much larger doses or parenteral vitamin D may be needed, and the
      underlying condition must be treated. Pseudofractures heal on imaging as the disease resolves.
      Because malabsorptive and renal osteomalacia need tailored dosing, this is not a
      self-management condition.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Unexplained widespread bone pain with muscle weakness
      deserves a proper work-up — 25(OH)D, calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and PTH — rather
      than assuming it is deficiency. Osteomalacia is very treatable once correctly identified.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-osteomalacia" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
